[Music]  Mexicans are murderers and rapists black  kids are super predators gun owners are  monsters and witches Jews are rats  dehumanization is nothing new it's been  around forever and it's come from all  sides and yet it remains the single  greatest threat to America's social  fabric now I know some of you may be  thinking why is Noah the right messenger  for this and I've heard it all over the  last few weeks Noah you're white Noah  you're a man Noah you're straight just  go back to the golf course but that's  actually exactly why I'm here because I  believe that beginning to de-escalate  the risk from dehumanization starts with  a little bit of personal responsibility  so I'd like to share an idea with you  today and it's an idea that some of you  might disagree with but I hope you know  it comes from a place of wanting to  preserve this incredible 250 year-old  experiment with democracy that is the  United States of America it goes like  this perhaps that in beginning to repair  our social fabric from the bottom up  perhaps instead of leaning in to all of  the things that make us different all  the things that dross apart instead of  trying to take back all of the negative  stereotypes that exist about each and  every one of our identity groups just  maybe it's time to reimburse that old  idea of the great American melting pot  not to destroy identity but to build a  shared identity that is greater than the  sum of its parts  and I believe that the way to do that is  to engage with people on their values  and ideas on their merits now why do I  think this is an idea worth spreading  why is this a problem that urgently  needs addressing I actually don't think  you have to look too far to see it in  our last election we had one candidate  demonize an entire country we had  another candidate call half of the  voters of this country deplorable  tragedies like 9/11 brought an  incredible generation of Americans who  gave up established careers to stand  shoulder to shoulder in fighting for our  freedom in our flag today tragedies seem  to produce arguments about who or what  we should ban from this country but it's  not just politics the private sector  plays a role in this to think about the  different ways that you're asked to  define yourself more narrowly on  Facebook you have to summarize your  activities and interests on a page and  linked in all of your work and  accomplishments and a profile one  picture a one-line biography to help  people decide if they want to date you  the media is complicit in this too it's  gotten to the point where all that's  covered seems to be complete and utter  outrage or strict adherence to political  correctness and political correctness  comes from both sides the right is just  as politically correct at about things  like standing for the national anthem as  the left is about things like abortion  or immigration the result is the  creation of almost a pseudo religion  around certain ideas or groups so that  we can't even have serious conversations  about the social issues that matter most  to this country we know how this ends  we've seen it time and time again create  the other ostracize it vilify it and  destroy it it's happening in Myanmar  today it happened in Rwanda in the 1990s  and yes it happened in Europe in the 40s  we cannot stand to see America's social  fabric unravel now I'm not talking about  just top-down institution building I'm  talking about the things that we can  each do to stitch it back together from  the bottom up and that comes back to  this idea of engaging people on their  values and ideas on their merits so what  does that meant for me well it's meant  that I've tried to immerse myself in in  ideas and places that I wouldn't usually  go adding places on the left like the  Huffington Post  BuzzFeed places on the right like  Breitbart Drudge Report even as far  right as The Daily stormer from these  experiences and from conversations with  people in this room and those far beyond  I believe that there is a way to stitch  this fabric back together so let's start  by talking about what it means to engage  people on their values some of you may  know of a senator from Ohio by the name  of Rob Portman senator Portman is a  Republican he was historically quite  conservative and notoriously against gay  marriage he was being vetted as Mitt  Romney's potential running mate in 2012  around that time senator portman son  came out to him as gay now like any good  senator he waited for a politically  astute moment but eventually  eventually he came out and spoke very  powerfully on this issue he changed his  position and said I am doing so not as a  politician not as a conservative but as  a dad who loves his son a lot and wants  him to have the same opportunities that  his sister might have the values of  equality of opportunity the values of a  dad who loves his son a lot a parent who  loves their son a lot should resonate  with all of us if I'm to criticize  credibly I have to acknowledge  appropriately when I see the values that  I care for being embodied by our leaders  which brings me to the second part of  this solution what does it mean to  engage ideas on their merits and to that  I'm going to offer you all three  challenges to it the first put down the  pitchforks step back from the politics  of blame this is much easier said than  done I fall victim to this all the time  it it is so easy to vilify it is so easy  to express anger but the reality is it  doesn't work  it pushes people's backs against the  wall it makes them feel like they're  defending the closely-held identities  and beliefs it actually produces the  cycle of radicalization which leads me  to my second challenge for go labels see  if you can go a week without using words  that end in I st or I am those isint  isms they're easy to throw out how many  times you heard the conversation that's  racist no no I'm not racist or that  sexist no no no I'm not sexist it's not  that racist or sexist ideas need to go  without being called out but naming is  far less powerful than explaining it  produces that same  dynamic of radicalization which brings  me to my third and final challenge don't  be afraid of ideas if I can accept when  I'm in a classroom or I'm at work that I  am learning when I am challenged when I  am pushed beyond my comfort zone  why do I not feel the same way about my  political ideology what does this look  like well I'm hoping you all might model  that with me today some of the ideas  that I've shared came from things I've  read deep on the far left from the  radical Saul Alinsky or all the way here  on the right from provocateurs like Milo  you Napoles if I told you all that when  you walked into the room some of you  might have walked out but the idea is  that the people are far less important  than the ideas so if you agreed with  what I had to say or you liked it great  let's talk about it if you hated it or  you're angry at me right now  great let's talk about it but either way  we got to talk about it because I  believe that the single most important  thing that this generation can do is  keep this great multicultural democratic  experiment going thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

